# =============================================================================
# P2P Broadband — Caddy reverse proxy (HTTP/2 + HTTP/3)
#
# The bare `next start` server speaks HTTP/1.1 only. Browsers open many
# connections for ~26+ requests; HTTP/2 multiplexes them on one connection
# (Lighthouse audits ~730ms savings). Caddy gives you HTTP/2, and HTTP/3
# once TLS is on.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Point the site at localhost (browser hits Caddy instead):
#        echo -e 'P2P_HOST=127.0.0.1\nP2P_PORT=8080' > /etc/p2p-broadband/env
#        systemctl restart p2p-broadband         # next start now on 127.0.0.1:8080
#   2. Install Caddy:  apt install -y caddy
#      (or:  curl -fsSL https://caddyserver.com/install.sh | sudo bash)
#   3. Copy THIS file to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile and: systemctl restart caddy
#   4. Keep port 3000 open on the firewall (Caddy listens there now).
#
# Modes:
#   A) Without a domain (bare IP, no TLS): HTTP/2-clear (h2c). Firefox and
#      Node.js clients get multiplexed HTTP/2; Chrome only uses HTTP/2 over
#      TLS, so for maximal Chrome benefit use mode B.
#   B) With a public hostname: uncomment the "p2pbroadband.in" site block,
#      disable mode A, and Caddy auto-provisions HTTPS (HTTP/2 + HTTP/3).
#      A domain is required for Let's Encrypt certificates.
# =============================================================================

# --- Mode A: cleartext on the current IP:port ------------------------------
:3000 {
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}

# --- Mode B: domain + automatic HTTPS (HTTP/2 + HTTP/3) ---------------------
#p2pbroadband.in {
#	encode zstd gzip
#	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
#}

# Note: with mode B, switch Caddy's listener to 443 (default), forward 80->443,
# and open 443/tcp + 443/udp (HTTP/3) on the firewall.